WEBVTT

00:01.653 --> 00:04.721
♪ It seems today
that all you see ♪

00:05.100 --> 00:08.419
♪ Is violence in movies
and sex on TV ♪

00:08.470 --> 00:11.889
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:11.956 --> 00:14.842
♪ On which we used to rely? ♪

00:14.926 --> 00:18.462
♪ Lucky there's a family guy ♪

00:18.513 --> 00:21.649
♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do ♪

00:21.717 --> 00:23.250
♪ All the things that make us ♪

00:23.318 --> 00:24.702
♪ Laugh and cry ♪

00:24.770 --> 00:27.738
♪ He's... a...
Fam... ily... Guy! ♪

00:36.531 --> 00:39.114
Oh, my God, Brian, I went
to a baby rave last night.

00:39.117 --> 00:40.217
Dropped a
Flintstones chewable.

00:40.285 --> 00:41.452
It was a purple Betty.

00:41.520 --> 00:42.653
<i>I was flyin'!</i>

00:42.721 --> 00:44.105
I was up till
like... like...

00:44.172 --> 00:45.639
I don't even
know how late.

00:45.707 --> 00:47.892
<i>Like, when I came home,
The Mentalist was still on.</i>

00:47.959 --> 00:48.993
Like, that's how late.

00:49.061 --> 00:50.411
Morning, family.
Anyone needs me,

00:50.479 --> 00:52.980
I'll be out in the yard
using my new riding mower.

00:53.048 --> 00:54.849
A riding mower?
For God's sakes,

00:54.916 --> 00:56.951
stop making all these
frivolous purchases.

00:57.018 --> 00:58.552
You just blew
all that money

00:58.620 --> 01:00.805
hiring that English-
to-raccoon translator.

01:00.872 --> 01:02.673
(whispering
inaudibly)

01:02.741 --> 01:04.175
(chittering)

01:04.242 --> 01:05.876
(angry chittering)

01:05.944 --> 01:07.645
Don't need a translator
for that one.

01:07.712 --> 01:09.914
Tell him to come back when
he's ready to act like an adult.

01:14.252 --> 01:15.653
Hey, what's up, Quagmire?

01:15.720 --> 01:17.521
<i>Oh, there's the
sound of the birds.</i>

01:17.589 --> 01:19.023
I thought they
all disappeared,

01:19.091 --> 01:20.958
because all I could
hear was a lawnmower.

01:21.026 --> 01:22.093
Oh, and what's that?

01:22.160 --> 01:23.427
The wind in the trees!

01:23.495 --> 01:24.695
Good-- that
still exists.

01:24.763 --> 01:26.464
What are you doing there,
planting some flowers?

01:26.531 --> 01:28.599
Hey, look, why don't you
go bother Joe, huh?

01:28.667 --> 01:30.334
I can't. He's in Vegas
for the weekend,

01:30.402 --> 01:32.369
seeing that handicapped
Cher impersonator.

01:32.437 --> 01:36.640
♪ If I could turn back time ♪

01:36.708 --> 01:40.394
♪ If I could find a way... ♪

01:40.462 --> 01:43.230
<i>Yeah! We all want
to turn back time!</i>

01:43.315 --> 01:46.217
This is the biggest
sitting ovation I've ever seen!

01:49.187 --> 01:51.021
Damn it, where the hell
did I put that?

01:51.089 --> 01:52.306
What are you
looking for, Brian?

01:52.374 --> 01:54.058
That steak bone you
gave me last night.

01:54.126 --> 01:56.160
I can't remember what
the hell I did with it.

01:56.228 --> 01:57.511
Didn't you
bring it outside?

01:59.748 --> 02:00.414
(clattering)

02:00.499 --> 02:02.082
(screams)

02:02.150 --> 02:03.884
Holy crap! Quagmire,
you all right?

02:03.952 --> 02:05.319
No, I'm not all right!

02:05.387 --> 02:07.188
My head's gushing
blood, you idiot!

02:07.255 --> 02:08.522
Take your hand away.

02:08.590 --> 02:11.625
Oh, boy! All right,
hold still, Quagmire.

02:11.683 --> 02:12.525
(screams)

02:12.550 --> 02:14.475
Oh, my God, a piece
of your brain came out!

02:14.479 --> 02:15.830
Aah! Well, that
can't be good!

02:15.897 --> 02:17.298
Well, maybe
it's not so bad.

02:17.365 --> 02:19.250
I can feed it
to my pet zombie.

02:19.317 --> 02:20.751
Here you go.

02:20.819 --> 02:22.653
Yeah, you're hungry,
aren't ya?

02:22.721 --> 02:24.004
He was a rescue.

02:24.072 --> 02:26.674
When I found him,
he was just walking face-first

02:26.741 --> 02:28.375
into a chain-link fence
downtown.

02:32.447 --> 02:34.231
All right, almost
done here, Glenn.

02:34.299 --> 02:36.167
There we are,
all finished.

02:36.234 --> 02:37.935
Well, look at this.

02:38.003 --> 02:39.803
Look what happened.
(chuckles)

02:39.871 --> 02:42.275
Well, by the laws of
comedy, you and I have

02:42.276 --> 02:43.453
to be roommates now.

02:43.458 --> 02:45.576
That's hilarious, Doc.
All right, here we go.

02:45.644 --> 02:48.062
Now, I'm going to prescribe
a course of antibiotics

02:48.129 --> 02:50.614
and some painkillers and...

02:50.682 --> 02:52.016
Hey, Elmer.

02:52.083 --> 02:54.185
Hey.

02:54.252 --> 02:55.786
She sucks.

02:59.674 --> 03:01.358
Okay, Quagmire, before
we get your prescription,

03:01.426 --> 03:02.593
let's grab some bandages.

03:02.661 --> 03:04.461
God, this is such
an East Coast pharmacy.

03:04.529 --> 03:06.809
How are we gonna find
gauze and bandages in the

03:06.834 --> 03:07.998
middle of all this crap?

03:07.999 --> 03:10.022
Uh, I think he keeps them
next to the thousand-piece

03:10.047 --> 03:11.247
puzzle of a lobster trap.

03:11.303 --> 03:13.704
I don't see it. You mean,
next to the Kadima paddles,

03:13.772 --> 03:16.507
or next to the basic black
sweatshirt with no writing on it?

03:16.575 --> 03:18.071
No, I think they're over
there between the rain

03:18.096 --> 03:19.677
ponchos and the cap guns

03:19.678 --> 03:21.378
and just below the
balsa wood gliders

03:21.446 --> 03:24.048
and the net-bag of flip-flops
and a beach pail.

03:24.115 --> 03:25.261
Above the set of jacks?

03:25.286 --> 03:28.219
Yes, above the jacks and the
bicentennial playing cards

03:28.220 --> 03:30.654
and across from the giant
cage with the beach balls,

03:30.722 --> 03:33.190
next to the Wiffle ball bats
and the spark-spitting cars.

03:33.258 --> 03:36.126
I don't... Oh, next to the
wax bottles of weird juice.

03:36.194 --> 03:39.163
Yes, behind the plastic bag of
Mexican soldiers from the Alamo

03:39.231 --> 03:40.931
and the Super Elastic
Bubble Plastic.

03:40.999 --> 03:42.366
Oh, yeah, I got 'em.

03:42.434 --> 03:43.701
Hey, Mort.
How's it going?

03:43.768 --> 03:45.185
Oh, it's awful, Peter!

03:45.253 --> 03:46.086
Why? What's the matter?

03:46.154 --> 03:47.555
The pharmacy's going under.

03:47.622 --> 03:49.390
I can barely pay my bills.

03:49.457 --> 03:51.759
I can't even afford
the real pharmacy music.

03:51.826 --> 03:54.078
That's just me up there. Listen.

03:54.145 --> 03:56.073
MORT (a cappella):
♪ If this is it, ooh, wah,

03:56.098 --> 03:58.339
please let me know ♪

03:58.850 --> 04:02.586
♪ If this ain't love,
you better just say so ♪

04:02.654 --> 04:04.088
♪ If this is it... ♪

04:04.155 --> 04:06.423
They say I've got
Jewish honey in my voice.

04:06.491 --> 04:08.092
Jewish honey is mucus.

04:08.159 --> 04:09.643
But I thought you made
a lot of money here.

04:09.711 --> 04:11.812
I used to,
but it's all gone downhill

04:11.880 --> 04:13.414
since Muriel died.

04:13.481 --> 04:15.583
She could get away
with overcharging people,

04:15.667 --> 04:18.102
because they were always
mentally undressing her

04:18.169 --> 04:19.536
and then re-dressing her

04:19.604 --> 04:21.839
once they saw
the horrible mess underneath.

04:21.906 --> 04:24.575
But in that interval,
she robbed them blind.

04:24.643 --> 04:27.411
(sobbing)

04:27.479 --> 04:28.846
Ooh.

04:28.913 --> 04:30.080
Well, look, uh,
I don't know,

04:30.148 --> 04:31.315
maybe there's
something we can do

04:31.383 --> 04:32.783
to help you drum up
some business.

04:32.851 --> 04:33.884
You know, get
the word out.

04:33.952 --> 04:35.019
Like what?

04:35.086 --> 04:37.271
Hey, you just
leave that to us.

04:37.339 --> 04:39.173
(lively pop music playing)

04:39.240 --> 04:42.476
(register ringing
in rhythm with music)

04:42.544 --> 04:45.446
♪ ♪

04:47.549 --> 04:49.416
(rhythmic ringing
and music crescendo)

04:49.484 --> 04:50.551
(music stops)

04:50.619 --> 04:53.687
(register ringing rhythmically)

04:53.755 --> 04:55.706
Is opening and closing
the cash register

04:55.774 --> 04:57.641
a bunch of times
helping business yet?

04:57.709 --> 04:59.510
No.
Okay, then we'll do other stuff.

05:01.279 --> 05:03.180
Okay, Mort, now, if you
want to get more customers

05:03.248 --> 05:05.115
in your pharmacy, you're
gonna have to do a promotion,

05:05.183 --> 05:07.151
like... like
"buy one, get one free."

05:07.218 --> 05:09.153
What?
Buy one, get one free.

05:09.220 --> 05:10.454
Huh?
Buy one...

05:10.522 --> 05:12.256
Yeah, I get that.
Get one free.

05:12.324 --> 05:14.491
Is that, like, a Spanish word?
No, it's English.

05:14.559 --> 05:15.630
The whole thing's English.

05:15.655 --> 05:17.193
Oh, 'cause it seems
like you're saying

05:17.195 --> 05:19.930
the first part in English
and then some kind of made-up

05:19.981 --> 05:21.849
pha-lumpf-phalumpf
or something in the second part.

05:21.916 --> 05:23.203
I don't understand you.

05:23.228 --> 05:25.168
All right, let me try
explaining it another way.

05:25.170 --> 05:26.870
Let's say we're at
someone else's store,

05:26.938 --> 05:29.640
and there's a sign, and it says:
"Buy one, get one free."

05:29.708 --> 05:31.175
Get one free? Wow!

05:31.242 --> 05:33.010
What store are
you talking about?

05:36.464 --> 05:39.266
Excuse me, I have been
waiting for my prescription

05:39.334 --> 05:40.768
for 25 minutes!

05:40.835 --> 05:42.670
Oh, I'm really sorry,
Ms. Dumott.

05:42.737 --> 05:45.005
We'll have that filled
for you right away.

05:45.073 --> 05:47.374
Guys, what the hell's
going on back there?

05:47.442 --> 05:50.394
Remember, each pill has
to go into the correct bottle.

05:50.462 --> 05:52.780
(mechanical whirring)

05:54.015 --> 05:56.633
Eh, this ain't so hard.

05:56.685 --> 05:58.852
(whirring rises in pitch)

06:01.139 --> 06:03.807
(whirring increases in pitch
as conveyor belt speeds up)

06:06.911 --> 06:09.113
(whirring intensifying)

06:13.484 --> 06:14.918
(vomits)

06:14.986 --> 06:18.088
(woozily): I...
I got a wicked boner.

06:18.156 --> 06:19.306
(thudding)

06:23.044 --> 06:24.344
This was a great
idea, Peter.

06:24.429 --> 06:25.713
We fly this thing
over Providence,

06:25.797 --> 06:27.497
it's bound to get people
into the pharmacy.

06:27.549 --> 06:29.099
That's right, bring
in the city folk

06:29.167 --> 06:31.168
with their big money
and their long cigarettes

06:31.219 --> 06:33.053
and their dogs with shoes.

06:33.138 --> 06:34.192
Hey, check it out--

06:34.217 --> 06:36.314
we got a giggity over
there on the 32nd floor.

06:36.507 --> 06:38.559
I'm gonna go in
for a closer look.

06:39.861 --> 06:42.613
QUAGMIRE:
Heh-heh, all right!

06:42.680 --> 06:44.514
(gasps)
Quagmire, look out!

06:44.566 --> 06:45.816
Oh, no!

06:49.154 --> 06:50.821
Geez, that was a close one!

06:50.872 --> 06:52.423
Yeah, but at least
nobody got hurt.

06:55.026 --> 06:56.860
(tires squealing)

06:59.164 --> 07:00.764
(clattering)

07:00.832 --> 07:03.333
(burbling)

07:03.401 --> 07:05.369
Goldman's Pharmacy!

07:05.437 --> 07:09.540
Now, that could be a good place
to get new shoes for my dog.

07:15.079 --> 07:16.313
Good evening, Quahog.

07:16.381 --> 07:18.582
Grim details continue
to roll in at this hour

07:18.650 --> 07:20.984
in the story of the detached
airplane advertising banner

07:21.052 --> 07:23.754
which caused a school bus to
plummet into the Pawtuxet River.

07:23.822 --> 07:24.955
That's right, Joyce.

07:25.023 --> 07:26.824
<i>Quahog's own Goldman's Pharmacy</i>

07:26.891 --> 07:28.558
<i>made headlines
across the country</i>

07:28.610 --> 07:32.362
<i>after a banner of theirs caused
the bus to drive off a bridge,</i>

07:32.413 --> 07:34.448
<i>tragically killing
all the teenagers on board.</i>

07:34.532 --> 07:36.533
<i>Many of the students
tweeted about the accident</i>

07:36.584 --> 07:37.835
<i>as it was happening.</i>

07:37.902 --> 07:39.086
<i>We have a few here.</i>

07:39.170 --> 07:40.954
<i>From @Jenny94:</i>

07:41.039 --> 07:43.474
<i>"Water seriously cold, yo."</i>

07:43.541 --> 07:46.193
<i>Here's @TheTed69:</i>

07:46.261 --> 07:49.379
<i>"I'm gonna survive this.
LOL jk."</i>

07:49.447 --> 07:51.114
"Jk," indeed.

07:51.182 --> 07:52.716
Oh, God, I'm ruined!

07:52.784 --> 07:55.185
Nobody's ever gonna shop
in my pharmacy again!

07:55.253 --> 07:56.553
I'm gonna lose
everything!

07:56.621 --> 07:58.422
Aw, gosh, Mort,
I feel terrible about this.

07:58.490 --> 08:00.808
I wish there was
some way we could fix it.

08:00.875 --> 08:03.076
You know, my cousin
had the same problem,

08:03.144 --> 08:06.029
but he lucked out, 'cause his
business burned to the ground.

08:06.097 --> 08:08.465
The insurance made him
a very rich man.

08:08.533 --> 08:10.734
(chuckles) Too bad we can't
just torch the pharmacy.

08:10.802 --> 08:12.469
Aw, come on, no,
we can't do that.

08:12.537 --> 08:14.238
That'd be like stealing
from the insurance companies.

08:14.305 --> 08:15.305
It wouldn't be right.

08:15.373 --> 08:16.540
Aw, they're bastards.

08:16.608 --> 08:19.076
Last year, after I lost
my beloved Muriel,

08:19.143 --> 08:22.513
our life insurance company
refused to pay on her policy.

08:22.580 --> 08:24.982
They took what should have been
a pleasant experience

08:25.049 --> 08:26.817
and made it into an ordeal!

08:26.885 --> 08:28.385
Well, that doesn't
totally surprise me.

08:28.453 --> 08:30.487
A friend of mine
staying in my house

08:30.555 --> 08:32.189
got, um, injured,

08:32.257 --> 08:35.259
and my homeowner's insurance
wouldn't pay her doctor bills.

08:35.326 --> 08:39.162
Said the policy didn't cover
an "act of rod."

08:39.230 --> 08:41.331
Yeah, come to think of it,
they wouldn't even pay

08:41.399 --> 08:43.567
for the fetus Lois lost
when we went to visit

08:43.635 --> 08:45.969
the Museum of Staircases
and Spring-loaded Boxing Gloves.

08:46.037 --> 08:48.238
Yeah, you know, they don't seem
to pay out on anything.

08:48.306 --> 08:50.307
No, not at all, right?
Yeah, that's true, they don't pay.

08:50.375 --> 08:52.242
Well, then I say,
screw the bastards!

08:52.310 --> 08:53.777
Let's burn
the place down!

08:53.845 --> 08:56.213
What?! Peter, we're talking
about a major crime here.

08:56.281 --> 08:59.166
This is not smuggling Oriental
women into the country in steel cans.

08:59.234 --> 09:00.701
This is a serious felony.

09:00.768 --> 09:02.402
Quagmire, this whole
mess was our fault.

09:02.470 --> 09:03.837
We got to make it right.

09:03.905 --> 09:05.739
And, look, as long
as nobody sees us,

09:05.807 --> 09:08.191
and we don't tell anybody,
then we're in the clear.

09:08.259 --> 09:11.028
Well, I guess I did kind of
destroy your business, Mort.

09:11.095 --> 09:13.230
All right, then let's do it--
tomorrow night!

09:13.298 --> 09:15.375
Good. I'm just gonna
have to reschedule my

09:15.400 --> 09:17.094
weekly re-circumcision.

09:17.302 --> 09:19.436
So, what are we doing today,
a little off the top?

09:19.504 --> 09:20.787
Yeah, but leave
a little up front

09:20.855 --> 09:22.472
so I have something to play with.
Sounds good.

09:22.540 --> 09:24.057
(unzips zipper) And take a look
at the back.

09:24.125 --> 09:25.192
You see that there?
Yeah.

09:25.260 --> 09:26.693
Yeah, let's trim that.
I don't know when

09:26.761 --> 09:28.061
that started, but
let's get rid of that.

09:31.266 --> 09:32.666
Okay, guys, listen up.

09:32.734 --> 09:34.468
This is a very
flammable mixture

09:34.535 --> 09:36.003
that's also
undetectable.

09:36.070 --> 09:39.072
If we put it on the electric box
and two of the outlets,

09:39.140 --> 09:42.459
then light them all at once,
it'll look like a short circuit.

09:42.527 --> 09:44.661
Geez, Mort, how the hell do
you know how to do all this?

09:44.729 --> 09:46.430
Oh, it's all covered in
the Torah.

09:46.497 --> 09:48.665
Right after the chapter on
writing complaint letters

09:48.733 --> 09:51.201
that will result in
compensatory merchandise.

09:51.269 --> 09:53.503
Dear stuffed shirts
at Ritz Crackers.

09:53.571 --> 09:56.607
A chain is only as strong
as its weakest link.

09:56.674 --> 09:59.660
Just as a box of crackers
is only as appealing

09:59.727 --> 10:01.945
as its least intact sleeve.

10:02.013 --> 10:04.247
I am paying
for fully formed crackers,

10:04.315 --> 10:05.983
not butter crumbles.

10:06.050 --> 10:08.251
I don't buy your product
with broken quarters;

10:08.319 --> 10:10.687
don't stick me
with broken pieces of cracker,

10:10.755 --> 10:12.356
you miserable dicks!

10:12.423 --> 10:14.725
Regards, Mort Goldman again.

10:23.084 --> 10:26.003
All right, let's get to work.

10:26.070 --> 10:28.005
♪ ♪

10:42.971 --> 10:45.188
(explosion)

10:45.256 --> 10:46.807
Oh, my God, we did it!

10:46.874 --> 10:48.625
Hey, Peter, what
are you gonna tell Lois

10:48.693 --> 10:50.110
when she asks
where you were tonight?

10:50.178 --> 10:51.912
Don't worry, I got
it all covered.

10:51.980 --> 10:53.847
(harsh braying)

10:53.915 --> 10:55.949
(screeching bray)

10:56.034 --> 10:58.368
Ha-ha!
This is awesome!

10:58.436 --> 11:00.537
And Peter has no idea!

11:08.272 --> 11:10.556
Well, Mr. Goldman,
we've ruled out foul play.

11:10.624 --> 11:13.076
It looks like it was just
a case of faulty wiring.

11:13.143 --> 11:14.911
Well, you're still gonna
have to buy something.

11:14.979 --> 11:16.813
This is rubble,
not a library.

11:16.880 --> 11:19.415
Okay, well...
I'm sorry for your loss,

11:19.483 --> 11:21.017
but the good news is
your insurance

11:21.085 --> 11:23.502
will be covering
all of your damages.

11:23.554 --> 11:26.255
You hear what he said, fellas?
Looks like we pulled it off.

11:26.323 --> 11:27.657
Yeah, for now.

11:27.725 --> 11:28.891
I still got a
bad feeling.

11:28.959 --> 11:30.560
He's gonna find something,
I just know it.

11:30.627 --> 11:32.762
Will you relax?
We got an ironclad alibi.

11:32.830 --> 11:35.531
<i>We were all seeing The Beatles
live at The Ed Sullivan Show.</i>

11:35.616 --> 11:39.035
(song ends,
audience screaming)

11:39.903 --> 11:40.887
(screams)

11:40.954 --> 11:42.722
PETER:
Sorry. I rode a bike here.

11:45.993 --> 11:48.861
All right, guys, all we got
to do now is keep a low profile

11:48.929 --> 11:50.897
and not mention a word
of this to anybody.

11:50.964 --> 11:52.398
Not even the police.

11:52.466 --> 11:53.900
That's right,
not a word to anyone.

11:53.967 --> 11:56.535
Nobody.
Lips are sealed.

11:56.587 --> 11:58.521
I want to tell somebody.

11:58.589 --> 12:00.023
Peter!
You shut your mouth!

12:00.090 --> 12:02.241
Hey, Mort, I, uh,
was really sorry

12:02.309 --> 12:03.409
to hear about
the pharmacy.

12:03.477 --> 12:04.977
Oh, thank you, Joe.

12:05.045 --> 12:06.779
I was very,
very sorry, too.

12:09.149 --> 12:10.400
Yeah, he seemed pretty smart.

12:10.467 --> 12:12.869
His shirt was tucked in,
so I bet he went to Harvard.

12:12.936 --> 12:14.303
Well, you're my
friend, Mort.

12:14.371 --> 12:16.406
I want you to know that
I'm going to personally

12:16.473 --> 12:17.707
go over all
the evidence.

12:17.775 --> 12:19.208
I'd hate to think
there's someone in town

12:19.276 --> 12:20.343
who's out to get you.

12:20.411 --> 12:21.944
Hey, Joe, they said
it was an accident.

12:22.012 --> 12:23.529
Roll away.
No, I'm just saying,

12:23.597 --> 12:25.198
as your friend, I
wouldn't feel right

12:25.265 --> 12:26.999
unless I left no
stone unturned.

12:27.067 --> 12:28.701
Geez, Joe, let it go.

12:28.769 --> 12:31.437
Yeah, Joe, even Robocop
took a day off once in a while.

12:33.273 --> 12:36.042
(phone ringing)

12:36.110 --> 12:37.110
Nope.

12:37.177 --> 12:38.344
(turns ringer off)

12:41.715 --> 12:44.217
I don't like Joe sticking
his nose into this.

12:44.284 --> 12:45.785
Maybe this was all
a big mistake.

12:45.853 --> 12:47.136
You're damn right it was.

12:47.221 --> 12:48.855
Why did I ever
agree to do this?

12:48.922 --> 12:50.173
This isn't who I am.

12:50.240 --> 12:52.375
I looked in the mirror
this morning, and it was like

12:52.443 --> 12:54.427
I was looking at a man
I'd never seen before.

12:54.495 --> 12:55.962
Hi.

12:56.029 --> 12:57.780
Scott Bakula.

12:57.848 --> 12:59.015
Just take it easy, Quagmire.

12:59.083 --> 13:00.416
There's nothing to worry about.

13:00.484 --> 13:02.085
(knocking)
JOE: Hey, it's Joe, open up.

13:02.152 --> 13:02.985
(all gasp)

13:04.204 --> 13:05.638
Uh, hey, Joe.

13:05.706 --> 13:06.706
What's going on?

13:06.774 --> 13:08.324
Oh, hey, I forgot,
I brought you guys

13:08.392 --> 13:10.276
souvenirs from
my trip to Vegas.

13:10.344 --> 13:14.280
They're T-shirts that say
"I lost my shirt in Lost Wages."

13:14.348 --> 13:17.183
(laughing)

13:26.093 --> 13:28.077
I think maybe
you should keep them.

13:28.145 --> 13:29.662
All right,
I'll see you guys later.

13:29.730 --> 13:32.715
I got to go see the forensics
team about the pharmacy.

13:32.783 --> 13:34.734
Don't you worry, Mort.

13:34.802 --> 13:37.436
We'll get to the bottom of this.

13:37.504 --> 13:38.771
I don't like
this, you guys.

13:38.839 --> 13:39.989
This is bad.

13:40.057 --> 13:41.841
Joe's gonna find out,
I just know it.

13:41.909 --> 13:43.309
Geez, Quagmire,
you need to relax.

13:43.377 --> 13:44.811
And I know
how to help.

13:44.878 --> 13:46.846
Calgon,
take him away.

13:46.914 --> 13:48.781
(harp arpeggio)

13:54.171 --> 13:55.788
QUAGMIRE: This is
worse, Peter!

13:55.856 --> 13:58.157
<i>Relax, this is supposed
to be your time.</i>

13:58.225 --> 14:00.326
This is the right way to
deal with your problems.

14:03.897 --> 14:05.832
All right, guys,
if Joe really is nosing around,

14:05.899 --> 14:09.068
I think I know how to get him
off the case once and for all.

14:09.136 --> 14:10.219
(dialing)

14:10.287 --> 14:11.454
JOE: Hello?

14:11.522 --> 14:13.523
Officer Swanson,
this is the chief of police.

14:13.590 --> 14:14.924
I'm afraid
you're fired.

14:14.992 --> 14:17.393
I see. Well,
that's too bad.

14:17.461 --> 14:18.828
I was looking forward

14:18.896 --> 14:21.164
to giving you
your birthday present next week.

14:21.231 --> 14:22.665
Oh.

14:22.733 --> 14:23.950
Apparently,
there was a mistake.

14:24.017 --> 14:25.284
Forget I called.

14:25.352 --> 14:27.703
Guys, I got to become chief
of police by next week.

14:30.123 --> 14:33.192
Chief, this just came in
for you from Officer Swanson.

14:34.511 --> 14:37.046
A Starbucks gift card?!

14:37.114 --> 14:39.432
This has been
an unproductive use of my time.

14:41.635 --> 14:43.102
Okay, I got it.

14:43.170 --> 14:45.104
Joe can't investigate
if he's distracted, right?

14:45.172 --> 14:48.591
Well, what's more distracting
than texts about dinner plans?

14:48.659 --> 14:51.360
Hmm. These burn ignition
patterns aren't consistent

14:51.428 --> 14:53.329
with an electrical fire.

14:53.397 --> 14:55.364
(phone chirps)

14:55.432 --> 14:57.533
"Would... love to."

14:57.601 --> 14:59.719
(phone chirps)

14:59.786 --> 15:05.524
"Probably...
about 7:00... or 7:30."

15:07.077 --> 15:09.111
(phone chirps, Joe sighs)

15:09.179 --> 15:11.113
"Any... of...

15:11.181 --> 15:14.016
those places would be... fine."

15:14.084 --> 15:15.818
(phone chirps)

15:15.886 --> 15:18.187
God! No way!

15:18.255 --> 15:20.172
"I don't know

15:20.240 --> 15:23.492
"Probably just
a collared shirt

15:23.560 --> 15:25.511
"and jeans.

15:25.578 --> 15:27.680
"Have you never been

15:27.747 --> 15:31.500
to... a restaurant before?"

15:31.568 --> 15:32.868
Oh, he's getting annoyed.
Look at that.

15:32.936 --> 15:35.421
He sure is. Okay, now
tell him you want a vegan place.

15:35.489 --> 15:36.856
(phone ringing)

15:36.924 --> 15:38.557
Oh, my God,
he's so pissed, he's calling.

15:38.625 --> 15:39.959
Well, answer it.
No, no, no.

15:40.027 --> 15:41.994
I'm gonna send it to voicemail,
then call him back,

15:42.062 --> 15:43.879
but pocket-dial him
while I walk a few blocks.

15:43.947 --> 15:45.781
JOE (muffled,
from Peter's phone): Peter?

15:45.866 --> 15:47.783
Peter? Hello?

15:47.868 --> 15:50.069
I'm getting
very little work done!

15:53.690 --> 15:55.591
You guys, I just can't
take this anymore.

15:55.659 --> 15:57.460
I've been giving it
a lot of thought,

15:57.527 --> 15:59.495
and I think
we all just need to confess.

15:59.563 --> 16:00.930
What? Now just
hold on, Quagmire.

16:00.998 --> 16:02.632
If you start yapping
to the cops,

16:02.699 --> 16:05.034
we're gonna rot in prison
for the next 30 years.

16:05.102 --> 16:06.202
JOE:
Evening, gentlemen.

16:06.270 --> 16:07.720
(all gasp)
Joe!

16:07.788 --> 16:09.021
Glad I found
you here.

16:09.089 --> 16:11.223
I had a question
for you guys.

16:11.291 --> 16:13.376
Did any of you happen
to be at Mort's pharmacy

16:13.443 --> 16:14.844
on the night of the fire?

16:14.911 --> 16:16.112
No.
None of us were.

16:16.179 --> 16:18.214
I'm triplets. Could have
been one of my triplets.

16:18.282 --> 16:20.283
I see. Well, then
it's kind of peculiar

16:20.350 --> 16:21.817
that I would
find this.

16:21.885 --> 16:23.686
A pocket-dial
voicemail from Peter

16:23.754 --> 16:25.454
on the night
of the fire.

16:25.522 --> 16:28.557
PETER: Oh, my God, look at that fire
we started, Quagmire and Mort.

16:28.625 --> 16:30.559
You know
who I don't miss is Joe.

16:30.627 --> 16:33.062
That's a piece of evidence
that hurts all of us.

16:33.130 --> 16:35.998
You three are under arrest for
burning down Goldman's Pharmacy.

16:36.066 --> 16:39.302
Well, the pocket-dial giveth,
and the pocket-dial taketh away.

16:39.369 --> 16:40.936
You'll never take me alive!

16:41.004 --> 16:42.705
Aren't you gonna run?

16:42.773 --> 16:45.875
I would, but I don't want
my gym teacher to laugh at me.

16:45.942 --> 16:48.361
He's been following me around
since junior high.

16:48.428 --> 16:50.463
Watch this.
Watch this Jewish kid run.

16:56.965 --> 16:58.966
First, I have to read you
the Miranda rights

16:59.034 --> 17:00.434
for Jewish people.

17:00.502 --> 17:03.003
You have the right to remain
silent, even though you won't.

17:03.071 --> 17:05.840
You have the right to infer an
insult where none was intended.

17:05.907 --> 17:07.374
What's that
supposed to mean?

17:07.442 --> 17:10.194
You have the right to a lawyer,
who you are probably related to.

17:10.262 --> 17:11.562
This interview
is over.

17:13.231 --> 17:15.132
What were you dointhe night
of March 8th?

17:15.200 --> 17:16.600
(Bleep) your mother.

17:16.668 --> 17:17.968
Come on, man,
that's not cool.

17:18.036 --> 17:20.137
Oh, yeah? Maybe this
is cool enough for you.

17:20.205 --> 17:22.072
(farting)

17:22.140 --> 17:23.174
(blows)

17:23.241 --> 17:24.875
It's gonna take
a lot more

17:24.943 --> 17:27.077
than a fart in my
face to make me...

17:27.145 --> 17:28.846
Whoa! Whoa.

17:28.914 --> 17:30.815
Yeah, and there's more
where that came from.

17:30.882 --> 17:33.017
(straining)

17:33.084 --> 17:34.552
(sighs)
No, there isn't.

17:35.954 --> 17:37.588
Where were you
the night of the fire?

17:37.656 --> 17:39.590
I was at the movies.
What did you see?

17:39.658 --> 17:40.958
<i>No Strings Attached.
How was it?</i>

17:41.026 --> 17:43.327
Sucked.
Okay, that checks out.

17:43.411 --> 17:45.880
Boy, that Ashton Kutcher
sure was a cad

17:45.947 --> 17:47.348
in that movie, wasn't he?

17:47.415 --> 17:48.682
Yeah, totally.

17:48.750 --> 17:50.251
Wrong! He had
a heart of gold.

17:50.318 --> 17:51.752
You're going down.

17:55.140 --> 17:57.174
Your husband's in here, ma'am.
Lois!

17:57.242 --> 17:58.742
Peter, what's going on?

17:58.810 --> 18:01.111
Is it true? Did you really
burn down Mort's pharmacy?

18:01.179 --> 18:02.546
Hi, Lois. Hi.

18:02.614 --> 18:04.815
Look, Lois, we were just trying
to help Mort.

18:04.883 --> 18:07.184
Peter, how could you do this?
You have a family.

18:07.252 --> 18:09.086
Did you ever stop
to think about us

18:09.154 --> 18:11.789
before you did something that
could send you away to prison?

18:11.857 --> 18:15.259
(sighs) For once, could you visit me
in jail and not criticize me?

18:15.327 --> 18:16.794
Wait a minute.

18:16.862 --> 18:18.996
This isn't Carvel,
you lying bitch.

18:19.080 --> 18:20.214
All right,
that's enough.

18:20.282 --> 18:21.982
Visiting time
is over.

18:22.050 --> 18:24.201
You know you guys are
gonna do time for this.

18:24.269 --> 18:26.303
Come on, Joe. You're really
gonna put us away?

18:26.371 --> 18:27.838
You think I
want to do this?

18:27.906 --> 18:30.875
You know how hard it's gonna be
for me to make new friends?

18:30.942 --> 18:33.510
Hey, do you think Chris is
old enough to be my friend?

18:33.595 --> 18:35.696
Old enough? Yes. But I wouldn't
bark down that alley.

18:35.764 --> 18:37.231
You frighten him.

18:37.299 --> 18:39.633
He thinks you're a Transformer
that broke halfway through.

18:39.701 --> 18:41.368
I get that a lot.
I understand.

18:41.436 --> 18:42.636
Look, Mort was
in trouble.

18:42.704 --> 18:44.572
We-we were just trying
to help him out.

18:44.639 --> 18:47.141
Well, insurance fraud is not
the way to help somebody out.

18:47.208 --> 18:49.977
Listen, Joe, insurance agencies
are all scumbags.

18:50.045 --> 18:51.345
They deserve to get hurt.

18:51.413 --> 18:52.780
You don't decide that.

18:52.847 --> 18:54.348
What you did is
against the law.

18:54.416 --> 18:55.549
It's easy for you, Joe.

18:55.617 --> 18:56.850
You don't know what it's like

18:56.918 --> 18:58.319
to be screwed over
by an insurance company.

18:58.386 --> 19:00.154
Oh, I don't, do I?

19:00.221 --> 19:02.790
Well, let me tell you
a little story.

19:02.857 --> 19:05.759
<i>It was hours after my accident.</i>

19:05.827 --> 19:08.028
<i>I was barely conscious
on the operating table,</i>

19:08.096 --> 19:10.931
<i>but I'll never forget the phone
call that changed my life.</i>

19:10.999 --> 19:14.635
Hello, I'm calling on behalf of
your policyholder, Joe Swanson.

19:14.703 --> 19:16.737
He's badly injured his spine.

19:16.805 --> 19:18.339
Well, there's
a new procedure that,

19:18.406 --> 19:20.074
if administered immediately,

19:20.141 --> 19:22.076
should restore
his ability to walk.

19:22.143 --> 19:24.645
$200,000.

19:24.713 --> 19:27.247
We also have a wheelchair.

19:27.315 --> 19:29.617
$60.

19:29.684 --> 19:31.652
Okay.

19:31.720 --> 19:34.104
They're going with "B."

19:34.189 --> 19:36.657
Those sons of bitches
made it about money.

19:36.725 --> 19:38.626
Yeah, Joe.
So, what do you say?

19:38.693 --> 19:39.927
They didn't
care about you.

19:39.995 --> 19:41.428
Why should you
care about them?

19:41.496 --> 19:43.497
The thing is,

19:43.565 --> 19:45.766
you guys committed a crime,

19:45.834 --> 19:48.268
and I've still got the evidence
to prove it.

19:48.336 --> 19:49.870
Too bad I lost it.

19:49.938 --> 19:51.872
FEMALE AUTOMATED VOICE:
Message deleted.

19:51.940 --> 19:55.142
Was that... was that supposed
to mean something?

19:55.210 --> 19:56.977
Yeah, I'm letting
you guys go.

19:57.045 --> 19:58.445
Oh. Oh, well,
that's great.

19:58.513 --> 19:59.947
Yeah, I didn't
get that, either.

20:00.015 --> 20:01.665
Yeah, why didn't you
just say that?

20:01.733 --> 20:03.434
I'm starting to regret
erasing that message.

20:03.518 --> 20:04.718
Too late, butt-face.

20:04.786 --> 20:06.337
Have a great summer, Joe.

20:11.576 --> 20:12.609
Peter, I don't
understand

20:12.677 --> 20:14.578
how they just
let you out of jail.

20:14.646 --> 20:16.981
Yeah, Joe had all kinds of
evidence against you guys.

20:17.048 --> 20:18.882
Seems like a huge
miscarriage of justice.

20:18.950 --> 20:20.718
I thought if you do
something wrong,

20:20.785 --> 20:22.219
you're supposed
to get punished.

20:22.287 --> 20:24.238
(laughing):
Oh, Chris, not if you're white.

20:24.305 --> 20:26.707
Well, now that I've wrapped
things up with you guys,

20:26.775 --> 20:29.410
I better wrap things up
with my basement family.

20:29.477 --> 20:31.245
Well, did you guys
learn your lesson

20:31.312 --> 20:32.680
about eating
uncooked antelope?

20:34.115 --> 20:35.733
(hisses)

20:35.734 --> 21:36.734
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru